te $ 4 = 2^2 $ and $ 8 = 2^3 $:\n\n$$\n(2^2)^x = 2^3\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Simplify the left-hand side\n\n$$\n(2^2)^x = 2^{2x}\n$$\n\nSo now we have:\n\n$$\n2^{2x} = 2^3\n$$\n\nSince the bases are the same, we can equate the exponents:\n\n$$\n2x = 3\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Solve for $ x $\n\n$$\nx = \\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\n---\n\n### \u2705 Final Answer:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}\n$$\n\nSo, the power of 4 that equals 8 is $ \\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}} $.", "diagnostic_seconds": 7.47786465100944}
